# tests/backrefs/d27/gating.rxt -- D27 blinded acceptance corpus, module
# `backrefs`, the MODULE-DISABLED refusal direction of the gate, plus a
# few module-ENABLED cells kept here for CLI-surface continuity now that
# `backrefs` has landed (2026-08-22, module landing at main 3aa446f).
# Unlike every other file in this corpus, every cell here HAS been run
# against real `build/pcrec` (the brief's explicitly permitted exception:
# "you may run it only to confirm refusal-direction cells"; the
# module-enabled cells below were additionally verified against libpcre2
# for their match semantics, since they now assert real behavior, not
# just refusal). `perr` only asserts nonzero exit, never diagnostic
# wording (docs/testing.md), so no `perr` cell here depends on pcrec's
# exact message text -- only on the fact that it refuses.
#
# HISTORY WORTH KEEPING: before the module landed, cells 2, 7, and 10
# below were `perr` blocks asserting the MODULE-ENABLED-BUT-UNIMPLEMENTED
# refusal direction ("module 'backrefs' is enabled but ... is not
# implemented yet"). Once the module landed, those three patterns started
# compiling successfully with the same `features` lines, which is
# EXACTLY the intended outcome -- module landing turning a "not
# implemented yet" refusal into a real answer is success, not a corpus
# bug -- so per merge-review instruction they were converted in place to
# real `m`/`gp` cases, verified against libpcre2. The MODULE-DISABLED
# cells (1, 3, 4, 6, 8, 9, 11) are untouched: disabling a built module via
# `--features` still refuses today, by design, and always will.
#
# THE DUPNAMES/(?J) MODULE-ATTRIBUTION FINDING flagged in the pre-landing
# version of this file (and in dupnames.rxt) is now RESOLVED, per R32
# ASK-1: the landed module attributes BOTH `(?J)` and its duplicate-name
# RESOLUTION logic to `backrefs` (measured: `pcrec: inline option 'J'
# (dupnames) requires module 'backrefs'` -- no more mention of
# `named-groups` in that refusal). Cells 9 and 11 below are updated to
# match; their OUTCOME (still `perr`, for a different reason) was already
# correct pre-landing and needed no logic change, only comment upkeep.

# --- 1. Numeric backreference, module fully disabled. ---
pattern (a)\1
features classes
perr

# --- 2. Numeric backreference, module ENABLED -- landed 2026-08-22.
# Was `perr` (module enabled but unimplemented); now a real match cell,
# verified against libpcre2 (also re-checked end-to-end against the
# refreshed build/pcrec by hand: compiles, matches). ---
pattern (a)\1
features backrefs
m "aa" 0 2
gp 1 0 1
n "ab"

# --- 3. Octal/backref escape \8, module disabled -- confirms the gate
# applies to the ESCAPE RECOGNITION itself, before the octal-vs-backref
# disambiguation (F4) is ever reached. ---
pattern \8
features classes
perr

# --- 4. \g (any bracket form), module disabled. ---
pattern (a|b)\g<1>
features classes
perr

# --- 5. \g<1>: with `backrefs` enabled, the refusal reason CHANGES
# (measured, post-landing: "\g requires module 'recursion'", not a
# backrefs-unimplemented message) but the OUTCOME is unchanged --
# still `perr`. \g<name>/\g'name' are SUBROUTINE CALLS (F6, module
# `recursion`, unbuilt), not backreferences, and pcrec's own registry now
# reflects that attribution directly rather than folding \g<...> under
# `backrefs`'s own recognition gate the way it did pre-landing; see
# spellings.rxt's out-of-scope note for why match SEMANTICS of
# \g<name>/\g'name' are not tested by this corpus regardless. ---
pattern (a|b)\g<1>
features backrefs
perr

# --- 6. \k, module disabled. ---
pattern (?<n>a)\k<n>
features classes,named-groups
perr

# --- 7. \k, module ENABLED (plus named-groups) -- landed 2026-08-22.
# Was `perr`; now a real match cell, verified against libpcre2. ---
pattern (?<n>a)\k<n>
features backrefs,named-groups
m "aa" 0 2
gp 1 0 1
n "ab"

# --- 8. (?P=n), module disabled. ---
pattern (?P<n>a)(?P=n)
features classes,named-groups
perr

# --- 9. (?J), with modifiers present, and NO duplicate name anywhere in
# the pattern at all. MEASURED both before and after the module landed:
# `(?J)` refuses unconditionally, once its own syntax is recognized
# (module `modifiers`), REGARDLESS of whether a collision is ever
# actually declared -- pre-landing the refusal named `named-groups`;
# post-landing (R32 ASK-1) it names `backrefs` directly ("inline option
# 'J' (dupnames) requires module 'backrefs'"). The OUTCOME (perr, with
# `modifiers` alone) is unchanged either way; `backrefs` is still absent
# from this cell's feature list on purpose, to keep testing the same
# thing cell 11 tests from the other side. ---
pattern (?J)a
features modifiers
perr

# --- 10. (?J) with a genuine duplicate name, modifiers, named-groups AND
# backrefs all present -- module ENABLED, landed 2026-08-22 (R32 ASK-1
# attributes DUPNAMES resolution to `backrefs`). Was `perr`; now a real
# match cell (F9's core resolution rule: \k<a> picks the FIRST member of
# the name-run that is set), verified against libpcre2. ---
pattern (?J)^(?<a>x)(?<a>y)\k<a>$
features modifiers,named-groups,backrefs
m "xyx" 0 3
gp 1 0 1
gp 2 1 2
n "xyy"

# --- 11. (?J) with modifiers EXCLUDED (an explicit, non-default feature
# list that leaves modifiers out) refuses on the (?J) SYNTAX itself,
# before dupname resolution is ever reached -- a different refusal SITE
# than cell 9's (module `modifiers`'s own gate vs., pre-landing, module
# `named-groups`'s dupname refusal / post-landing, module `backrefs`'s
# own "requires module 'backrefs'" via cell 9 above once modifiers is
# present but backrefs is not). Both `perr`, and the .rxt format can't
# see the difference in wording, but the comment trail is worth keeping.
# Outcome unaffected by the R32 ASK-1 landing -- `backrefs` is present
# here but `modifiers` is not, so the (?J) syntax gate itself is what
# fires, same as pre-landing. ---
pattern (?J)(?<a>x)(?<a>y)
features backrefs,named-groups
perr
